//! A second, related consequence of this same narrow scope: the engine itself
//! never populates `write_sealed` on a rotated node's republished
//! `PublishedNode` (see `engine.rs::seal_and_publish`) — a read-key rotation
//! is a read-plane operation. Left unpreserved, that write-plane hole is a
//! CORRECTNESS bug, not merely a deferral: a rotated node republished with
//! `write_sealed: None` can no longer be resolved by ANY owned walk
//! (`list_folder_owned` fails closed on the missing write body — see
//! `crates/sdk/src/listing.rs`), so every subsequent owned readdir/refresh of
//! a folder CONTAINING a rotated node fails, and on a fresh mount `replay.rs`
//! cannot recover the node's signing seed (the seed lives in the write body).
//! macOS FUSE-T/SMB re-enumerates directories far more aggressively than
//! kernel-FUSE/WinFsp, so it surfaces this as a persistent owned-walk failure
//! (e.g. a covered overwrite-rename that never lands). This adapter therefore
//! PRESERVES the write plane on publish: [`rebuild_rotated_write_sealed`]
//! re-seals the node's own (rotation-independent) write body under its stable
//! write key at the NEW generation, from the same in-memory `InodeTable` this
//! module already sources the signing seed from — so a rotated node stays
//! owned-walkable and replay-recoverable. Full write-key *rotation* remains a
//! separate Phase-72 follow-up; this only re-seals the UNCHANGED write plane
//! at the bumped generation.

